WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.093
This is the first elective.

2
00:00:05.093 --> 00:00:08.505
A graph records who is connected to whom

3
00:00:08.505 --> 00:00:11.754
friends in a network, linked web pages

4
00:00:11.754 --> 00:00:13.460
neighbouring pixels.

5
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.192
Graphs often hide groups.

6
00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:18.670
Can one matrix find them?

7
00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.290
Pause and guess.

8
00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.683
Eight students form two study groups.

9
00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:29.344
Students four and five are friends

10
00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.789
across the groups

11
00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:33.830
that one friendship is the only bridge.

12
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.917
The adjacency matrix W has a

13
00:00:37.015 --> 00:00:39.361
one for each friendship

14
00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:42.391
and the degree matrix D counts

15
00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:44.737
each student's friends.

16
00:00:44.737 --> 00:00:48.940
The graph Laplacian is L equals D minus W.

17
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.756
Student four has three friends

18
00:00:51.756 --> 00:00:53.745
a three on the diagonal

19
00:00:53.745 --> 00:00:56.370
and a minus one for each friend.

20
00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.062
Every row adds to zero

21
00:00:59.062 --> 00:01:02.734
so L times the all ones vector is zero.

22
00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:06.590
The constant pattern has eigenvalue zero.

23
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.599
In Lecture eleven

24
00:01:08.599 --> 00:01:11.457
this matrix was A transpose A

25
00:01:11.457 --> 00:01:14.037
for the incidence matrix A.

26
00:01:14.037 --> 00:01:18.093
So x transpose L x adds up, over all edges

27
00:01:18.093 --> 00:01:21.872
the squared change of x across the edge.

28
00:01:21.872 --> 00:01:23.900
It is never negative.

29
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.613
Put plus one on one group

30
00:01:26.613 --> 00:01:29.158
and minus one on the other.

31
00:01:29.158 --> 00:01:32.248
Edges inside a group add nothing.

32
00:01:32.248 --> 00:01:36.247
The bridge adds two squared, which is four

33
00:01:36.247 --> 00:01:38.610
one edge cut, total four.

34
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:42.276
When is x transpose L x zero?

35
00:01:42.276 --> 00:01:46.852
Only when x is the same across every edge

36
00:01:46.852 --> 00:01:50.470
constant on each connected piece.

37
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.846
Remove the friendship.

38
00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:56.623
Now the eigenvalues are zero, zero, two

39
00:01:56.623 --> 00:01:58.557
two, and then fours

40
00:01:58.557 --> 00:02:00.860
one zero for each piece.

41
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.486
Put the friendship back.

42
00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:05.668
Only one zero remains

43
00:02:05.668 --> 00:02:09.841
but lambda two is just zero point two nine.

44
00:02:09.841 --> 00:02:14.014
Almost two pieces: lambda two is called the

45
00:02:14.109 --> 00:02:16.290
algebraic connectivity.

46
00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.620
Which pattern makes x transpose

47
00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:23.258
L x smallest, if it may not be constant?

48
00:02:23.258 --> 00:02:27.162
Ask for x perpendicular to the ones vector

49
00:02:27.162 --> 00:02:28.670
with length one.

50
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.045
By the Rayleigh quotient

51
00:02:31.045 --> 00:02:33.231
the minimum is lambda two

52
00:02:33.231 --> 00:02:35.499
reached at its eigenvector

53
00:02:35.499 --> 00:02:37.118
the Fiedler vector.

54
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:39.953
It is the smoothest pattern on the

55
00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:42.220
graph that is not constant.

56
00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.064
Which students get positive entries

57
00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:47.072
and which get negative ones?

58
00:02:47.072 --> 00:02:48.250
Pause and guess.

59
00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:52.747
Here it is.

60
00:02:52.747 --> 00:02:55.666
Students one to four are positive

61
00:02:55.666 --> 00:02:58.334
and five to eight are negative

62
00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:01.670
the signs recover the two study groups.

63
00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.458
The sizes say more.

64
00:03:03.458 --> 00:03:05.275
Students four and five

65
00:03:05.275 --> 00:03:07.319
at the ends of the bridge

66
00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:09.363
have the smallest entries

67
00:03:09.363 --> 00:03:11.937
and one and eight, farthest away

68
00:03:11.937 --> 00:03:13.300
have the largest.

69
00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:14.780
Why it works

70
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.694
the plus and minus one labels have zero sum

71
00:03:18.694 --> 00:03:22.346
so lambda two is at most four over eight

72
00:03:22.346 --> 00:03:23.215
one half.

73
00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:25.476
The Fiedler vector is the

74
00:03:25.563 --> 00:03:27.563
best relaxed labelling

75
00:03:27.563 --> 00:03:29.650
and its signs round it.

76
00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.686
A tempting shortcut

77
00:03:31.759 --> 00:03:34.306
take the eigenvector of the largest

78
00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:35.762
eigenvalue instead.

79
00:03:35.762 --> 00:03:38.310
Large means important, doesn't it?

80
00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.447
Here the largest is four point nine

81
00:03:41.447 --> 00:03:44.930
and its eigenvector is the roughest pattern

82
00:03:45.007 --> 00:03:47.175
changing as much as possible

83
00:03:47.252 --> 00:03:48.491
along the edges.

84
00:03:48.491 --> 00:03:50.039
Split by its signs

85
00:03:50.039 --> 00:03:52.980
and nine of the eleven edges are cut.

86
00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.633
For the Laplacian

87
00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:57.890
the groups live in the smallest eigenvalues.

88
00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.331
Now a graph with no groups at all

89
00:04:01.331 --> 00:04:04.361
all eight students know each other.

90
00:04:04.361 --> 00:04:07.896
Its eigenvalues are zero, and then eight

91
00:04:07.896 --> 00:04:08.990
seven times.

92
00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.133
Lambda two is large

93
00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:13.553
so there is no near split.

94
00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:15.435
It is also repeated

95
00:04:15.435 --> 00:04:18.841
so there is no single Fiedler vector

96
00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:20.992
and signs mean nothing.

97
00:04:21.082 --> 00:04:24.040
A gap after the small eigenvalues

98
00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.460
is what says groups exist.

99
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.734
So: the Laplacian is D minus W

100
00:04:30.853 --> 00:04:35.501
and x transpose L x adds up the squared

101
00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.480
changes along the edges.

102
00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:42.176
Its zero eigenvalues count the pieces

103
00:04:42.176 --> 00:04:45.611
a small lambda two means almost split

104
00:04:45.699 --> 00:04:48.165
and the signs of the Fiedler

105
00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:50.190
vector give the split.

106
00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.573
In part two

107
00:04:51.659 --> 00:04:55.017
why the Fiedler vector prefers balanced

108
00:04:55.103 --> 00:04:57.341
cuts to merely small ones

109
00:04:57.427 --> 00:05:00.010
and how the same idea clusters

110
00:05:00.096 --> 00:05:02.420
points that k means cannot.
